. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H1 * 
.         
.         * My original analysis was planned as: 
.         * I run a fixed effects model. I assume that gender equality attitude
> s can be measured through an index created by two items (plj0621 and plj0622)
>  that ask about attitudes towards women working. 
.         * The variable is coded as 1 = strongly disagree and 7 = strongly agr
> ee. A higher value indicates more liberal gender equality attitudes.
.         * I run a fixed-effects model with standard errors clustered at the i
> ndividual level.
.         * The variable genderAtt is the dependent variable and the dummy vari
> able hadDaughter is the independent variable. 
.         * I also include year fixed effects in the initial model. If sample s
> ize proves too small they will be removed. 
.         * However, this was infeasible (I can provide the code upon request).
>  I had already noted this potential problem in the PAP. 
.         * Therefore I switched strategies and did the following: 
.         * I identified questions on gender equality attitudes that had a high
> er rate of responses, albeit only in 2018 (plh0357 and plh0359)
.         * I then used PSM to match participants on age at birth of first chil
> d, migration background, logged income, gender, number of children, level of 
> education
.         * I then performed an OLS regression uses the propensity scores
.         
.         * with weights 
.         reg genderAtt hadDaughter i.female c.age_at_birth i.migback_binary c.
> log_income c.numChildren i.edu [pw=_weight] if matched, vce(robust)
(sum of wgt is 15,890)

